    "q-99773": {
      "dom_id": "step-q99773",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "$\\frac{(-\\frac{7}{4})}{(-9)}=$",
      "worked_solution": "We can use either of the following two methods.\n\n**Method 1 - The Long Way**\n\nFirst, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-\\frac{7}{4})}{(-9)}=(-\\frac{7}{4})÷(-9)\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $-9$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $-\\frac{1}{9}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\frac{7}{4})÷(-9) & = \\\\ (-\\frac{7}{4})\\times (-\\frac{1}{9}) & = \\\\ \\frac{7\\times 1}{4\\times 9} & = \\\\ \\frac{7}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-\\frac{7}{4})}{(-9)}=\\frac{7}{36}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2 - The Quick Way**\nThe result will have the same numerator as the top fraction, and the denominator is just the product of the two denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(-\\frac{7}{4})}{(-9)} & = \\\\ \\frac{-7}{4\\times (-9)} & = \\\\ \\frac{-7}{-36} & = \\\\ \\frac{7}{36} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
